  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "72fr1uc",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Sources of Ancient Indian History"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Indus Valley Civilization (IVC) was a Bronze Age civilization known for its advanced urban planning, burnt brick structures, and sophisticated drainage systems.",
        "Major excavated sites include Harappa (Ravi River), Mohenjo-daro (Indus River), Lothal (Gujarat), Kalibangan (Rajasthan), and Dholavira (Gujarat).",
        "The economy was based on agriculture (wheat, barley), craft production (steatite seals, pottery, bead-making), and foreign trade with Mesopotamia (Meluhha).",
        "Religious artifacts indicate the worship of a Mother Goddess, Pashupati (Proto-Shiva), sacred trees (Pipal), and animals, with no evidence of dedicated temple structures.",
        "The civilization declined around 1900 BCE due to factors such as climate change, shifting river courses, ecological imbalance, and trade disruption."
      ],
      "id": "7kify2t"
    },
